0 WALDO~
Waldo is potentially useful for training new players.  He teleports
when any player finds him, providing a target to practice finding
rooms.

Waldo gives a random potion to players that are in the main war.

Note that the "where" command can be used on Waldo for free by
any player in or out of war.  "where waldo".

If you don't recognize the room name, use the "whereis" command
to find it.  e.g. "where hallway"

Right.  Clever.  Or not.
~


0 CHANGECLASS~
Thus I would ask that you use only one character to not clog up everything...

Syntax:  changeclass <three letter class>

The classes are as follows:
mag = mage     cle = cleric    war = warrior        thi = thief
ran = ranger   pal = paladin   ant = anti-paladin 
brd = bard     mon = monk      ass = assassin

These are not all the available classes.  Please type 'cview' and 
'civew (class)' to get more info.

Note that you may only changeclass when out of war, and that your armor
class is set to 100 (naked) when you use this command.

~

0 MAGE THIEF WARRIOR ANTI-PALADIN PALADIN RANGER ILLUSIONIST DEFILER~

Type: cview <class name or first three letters of class>
To get help on a class.
~

0 ASSASSIN ABJURER CONJURER ENCHANTER BARD NINJA CLERIC~

Type: cview <class name or first three letters of class>
To get help on a class.
~

0 INVOKER ALTERER NECROMANCER BARBARIAN PSIONICIST ASSASSIN~

Type: cview <class name or first three letters of class>
To get help on a class.
~

0 DIVINER~
The diviner is a specialist mage devoted to information style
spells.  The diviner is an oracle of information, and has the
cheapest mana cost for locate object, and is the only class with
the ability to get free wheres all the time.

No type of invisibility can hide a person from a diviner, and
neither can other sensory deprivation type spells, such as
blindness.
~

0 MONK~
The monk is the master of unarmed combat, and has no need for
weaponry.

His natural armor class is lower than most classes can get in
most wars, and his bare hand damage is quite respectable.

The monk has reasonably high regeneration, as well as a very
high constitution, which reduces the amount of damage he takes
slightly.

The monk may not wear equipment other than rings and bracelets.
~

0 TINTIN~

Tintin is not only allowed, but it is encouraged.  I do not intend on
adding aliases, or any sort of playing aid.  PLEASE NOTE however, that
tintin (and other clients) are used only for aliases, NOT for actions.
Tintin actions will get you slayed.  A lot.  

You can ftp tintin from the following ftp sites:
princeton.edu
ftp.tcp.com
ftp.math.okstate.edu
There are others, but I can not think of them at the moment.
You will need to:
gunzip tintin(whatever_version).tar.gz
tar -xvf tintin(whatever_version).tar
cd tintin(whatever)
make

Then you should have an executable... read the documentation that comes with
it rather than asking for help.

Note that while tintin is encouraged, multiple players are illegal.
Although I will not intentionally look out for multiple players, if it
is obvious you are doing it, you will be sitebanned.
(Grouping with real life friends is not technically illegal either,
but if it becomes a common trend, I think some _big_ mobs that hunt
grouping characters might be in order :)
~


0 DEATH~
When you die, you get credited, er, penalized with a death.  Your slayer
then gets the pickings of your goods and gets credit for a kill.  Once you 
are dead, you're dead.  Explore the mud, cheer on a player, read HELP STORY, 
and basically wait.  Dying is a good thing here, for the person that killed 
you anyways...
~



0 !~
Syntax: !

! repeats the last command you typed.
~

0 NORTH SOUTH EAST WEST UP DOWN~
Syntax: north
Syntax: south
Syntax: east
Syntax: west
Syntax: up
Syntax: down

Use these commands to walk in a particular direction.
~

0 CAST~
Syntax: cast <spell> <target>

Spell casting classes can see what spells they have by typing prac.  Each 
spell has a percentage attached to it, which represents your skill level for
that spell.  Casting spells costs mana. The mana cost can be seen with the 
'cview' function for that class. All spells you cast at a 30th level of 
proficiency.

The <target> is optional.  Many spells which need targets will use an
appropriate default target, especially during combat.

If the spell name is more than one word, then you must quote the spell name.
Example: cast 'cure critic' frag.  Quoting is optional for single-word spells.
You can abbreviate the spell name.

When you cast an offensive spell, the victim usually gets a saving throw.
The effect of the spell is reduced or eliminated if the victim makes the
saving throw successfully.

See also the help sections for individual spells.
~

0 EXITS~
Syntax: exits

Tells you the visible exits of the room you are in.  Not all exits are visible.

To automatically see exits, type 'config +autoexit'.  If you have not yet saved
you character, you will need to retype this each time you logon. (HELP CONFIG)

~


0 DROP GET GIVE PUT TAKE~
Syntax: drop <object>
Syntax: drop <amount> coins
Syntax: get  <object>
Syntax: get  <object> <container>
Syntax: give <object> <character>
Syntax: give <amount> coins <character>
Syntax: put  <object> <container>

DROP drops an object, or some coins, on the ground.

GET gets an object, either lying on the ground, or from a container, or even
from a corpse.  TAKE is a synonym for get.

GIVE gives an object, or some coins, to another character.

PUT puts an object into a container.

DROP, GET and PUT understand the object names 'ALL' for all objects and
'ALL.object' for all objects with the same name.
~

0 EQUIPMENT INVENTORY~
Syntax: equipment
Syntax: inventory

EQUIPMENT lists your equipment (armor, weapons, and held items).
INVENTORY lists your inventory.
~

0 COMPARE~
Syntax: compare <object-1> <object-2>
Syntax: compare <object>

COMPARE compares two objects in your inventory.  If both objects are weapons,
it will report the one with the better average damage.  If both objects are
armor, it will report the one with the better armor class.

COMPARE with one argument compares an object in your inventory to the object
you are currently wearing or wielding of the same type.

COMPARE doesn't consider any special modifiers of the objects.

COMPARE is based on standard Merc item stats, and may or may not give
you reliable results with normal Diku stats as I re-implemented...
~

0 BACKSTAB 'SIXTH SENSE'~
Syntax: <backstab/ba/bs> <char>

Backstab is for thiefly like chracters, you need a "stabber", that is a
piercing weapon. Then you type '<backstab/ba/bs> <victim>'.

Successfully hitting a backstab consists of 2 phases.  First, a skill
check must be performed to see if the backstab attempt was not failed
outright.  Second, a successful hitroll must be rolled to reach the
damage dealing phase.

Any character may backstab a sleeping character.

Sixth sense gives the intended victim of a backstab a slight premonition,
sometimes, and may cause more backstabs than usual to miss them.

The victim's sixth sense value is subtracted from the attacker's backstab
skill prior to the initial skill check.

Assassins can backstab characters that are already in combat.
~


0 DISARM~

Syntax: disarm

Disarm only works during combat, you can remove your opponents weapon
and cause it to fall to the floor.  You get a delay from this.

Monks have a %chance of destroying their opponents weapon if their disarm
is successful.

Anyone except a monk needs a weapon to disarm, and only certain weapons
may be suitable for disarming.

~

0 KICK~

Kick is just damage to the opponent, with a delay to the kicker.

Monks can destroy opponents armor with a kick, but just like disarm they
have a %chance of doing it.
~

0 KILL MURDER~

Syntax: kill <char>

These are probably the most useful commands in pkilling, heh.
You just type 'kill <opponent>' and combat will begin, however,
you are not required to type out the opponents whole name if they
are a player. You can abbreviate their name down tho the first
character.  So if you wanted to kill Borlak, you could just type
'kill b', but diku allows you to abbreviate commands as well, so
even 'k b' would work.

~

0 BALANCE BASH~

Syntax: bash <char>

Bash will put your opponent in a resting position, and delay both the
basher and the opponent for a couple of rounds.

A shield is required to make a successful bash, well, without a shield
you very unlikely to hit one. Also a weapon is needed.

The balance skill helps characters maintain their balance when someone
is attempting to bash them.  It directly counteracts bash skill.
~

0 FLEE RESCUE~
Syntax: flee
Syntax: rescue   <character>

Once you start a fight, you can't just walk away from it.  If the fight
is not going well, you can attempt to FLEE. 

RESCUE seems pretty silly on the mud, because war is every man for himself.


~

0 TRACK~
Track is automatic if you have the skill.  It tells you the last live
PC and the direction the left the room.
~

0 EXAMINE LOOK~
Syntax: look
Syntax: look    <object>
Syntax: look    <character>
Syntax: look    <direction>
Syntax: look    <keyword>
Syntax: look in <container>
Syntax: look in <corpse>
Syntax: look far (direction)
Syntax: examine <container>
Syntax: examine <corpse>

LOOK looks at something and sees what you can see.

EXAMINE is short for 'LOOK container' followed by 'LOOK IN container'.

LOOK FAR lets you look into the next room, if you have the ability, by
peek or the spell farsee.
~

0 ORDER~
Syntax: order <character> command
Syntax: order all command

ORDER orders one or all of your charmed followers (including pets) to
perform any command.  The command may have arguments.  You are responsible
for the actions of your followers, and others who attack your followers
will incur the same penalty as if they attacked you directly.

Most charmed creatures lose their aggresive nature (while charmed).
~

0 REST SLEEP STAND WAKE~
Syntax: rest
Syntax: sleep
Syntax: stand
Syntax: wake

Resting will regen you slightly faster then normal, but be wary because 
sitting on the ground makes you take 1.5* damage.

Sleeping is pretty much useless, since regen is the same as for resting, 
and when you are asleep you can't do 'where'.  Sleeping people take 2* damage.

sleep (spell)
Syntax: cast sleep <victim>

This spell puts its victim to sleep.

There is no duration to sleep.  If you have plans for them, do it quick.
However, the enchanter class casts sleep with a duration, but the victim
gets a second saving throw against the duration.
~

0 ; REPLY SAY TELL~
Syntax: say   <message>
Syntax: tell  <character> <message>

SAY sends a message to all awake players in your room.  The single quote '''
is a synonym for SAY.

TELL sends a message to one awake player anywhere in the world.

REPLY sends a message to the last player who sent you a TELL.  REPLY will work
even if you can't see the player, and without revealing their identity.  This
is handy for talking to invisible or switched immortal players.
~

0 NOTE~
Syntax: note list
Syntax: note read    <number>
Syntax: note read    all
Syntax: note +       <message>
Syntax: note subject <string>
Syntax: note to      <to-list>
Syntax: note clear
Syntax: note show
Syntax: note post
Syntax: note remove  <number>

NOTE LIST lists notes which you can read.  NOTE READ reads one or all notes.

NOTE TO < > defines the list of people who will receive the note: 'all' sends it
	to all players, 'imm' sends it to immortals only, or specify a name.
NOTE SUBJECT < > defines the subject heading for your note.
NOTE + < > writes a line of text for your note, each time you use note +, a new
	   line of your note is added.
NOTE SHOW shows you what your note looks like so far.
NOTE CLEAR erases you entire note (if you've posted it already, too bad).
NMOD FORMAT makes your note look nice (justifies it and stuff).
NOTE POST posts your note so others can read it.

NOTE REMOVE is used by immortals only.
~
0 AUCTION CHAT . MUSIC QUESTION ANSWER SHOUT YELL~
Syntax: auction  <message>
Syntax: chat     <message>
Syntax: music    <message>
Syntax: question <message>
Syntax: answer   <message>
Syntax: shout    <message>
Syntax: yell     <message>

These commands send messages through communication channels to other players.

AUCTION, CHAT, MUSIC, and QUESTION and ANSWER also send messages to all
interested players.  '.' is a synonym for CHAT.  The QUESTION and ANSWER
commands both use the same 'question' channel

SHOUT & YELL send a message to all awake players within your area.

To turn off any channel, type CHAN -<channel>
To turn it back on again, type CHAN +<channel>
(You can also turn off the mob/spy info channel with CHAN -INFO)
~

0 EMOTE , SOCIAL~
Syntax: emote <action>

EMOTE is used to express your unique emotions or actions.  Besides EMOTE, 
there are several dozen built-in social commands, such as CACKLE, HUG, 
and THANK.  (type socials to see the list).

~

0 HOLD REMOVE WEAR WIELD~
Syntax: hold   <object>
Syntax: remove <object>
Syntax: wear   <object>
Syntax: wear   all
Syntax: wield  <object>

Three of these commands will take an object from your inventory and start using
it as equipment.  HOLD is for light sources, wands, and staves.  WEAR is for
armor.  WIELD is for weapons.

WEAR ALL will attempt to HOLD, WEAR, or WIELD each suitable item in your
inventory.  (Certain items are not affected by wear all)

You may not be able to HOLD, WEAR, or WIELD an item is too heavy for you, or 
if you are not experienced enough to use it properly.

REMOVE will take any object from your equipment and put it back into your
inventory.
~

0 CHANNELS~
Syntax: channels
Syntax: channels +<channel>
Syntax: channels -<channel>

With no options, CHANNELS shows you your current channels.  With a plus or
minus sign and an option, CHANNELS turns that channel on or off.
~

0 CONFIG~
Syntax: config
Syntax: config +<option>
Syntax: config -<option>

This command configures some of your character behavior.  With no options,
CONFIG shows you your current settings.  With a plus or minus sign and
an option, CONFIG turns that option on or off.

The options are:

    AUTOEXIT  You automatically see exits.
    AUTOLOOT  You automatically loot corpses.
    AUTOSAC   You automatically sacrifice corpses.
autoloot and autosac don't do anything... do it yourself 
    BLANK     You have a blank line before your prompt.
    BRIEF     You see brief descriptions only.
    COMBINE   You see object lists in combined format.
    PROMPT    You have a prompt.
    TELNETGA  You receive a telnet GA sequence.
~

0 BUG IDEA TYPO~
Syntax: bug <message>
Syntax: idea <message>
Syntax: typo <message>

These commands will take your message and record it into a file as feedback
to the mud implementors.   I even changed em so they go into the log!
End up in two places, make me read em twice!!
~

0 CREDITS~
Syntax: credits

This command shows the list of the original Diku Mud implementors.
~

0 REPORT SCORE TIME WEATHER~
Syntax: report
Syntax: score
Syntax: time
Syntax: weather

REPORT doesn't work on this mud.

SCORE shows detailed statistics to you only.

TIME shows the game time, as well as the time the mud was last started,
and the current local time for the host computer.

WEATHER shows the current game weather.
~

0 areas COMMANDS SOCIALS~
Syntax: areas [enabled/disabled]
Syntax: commands
Syntax: socials

AREAS shows a list of all areas in the game.  With the optional parameters,
it will give a list of zones enabled or disabled for the current war.
The fields on areas are <author> <area name> <access percent>.

COMMANDS shows you all the (non-social) commands available to you.

SOCIALS shows you all the social commands available to you.
~

0 LAST~
Syntax: last

This command shows recent mortal logins.
~

0 WHO~
Syntax: who

WHO shows the people currently in the game.  Some people may choose to
remain unseen and won't appear on the WHO

[rating K/D Win% Doom% class]

The first column is rating, which is a rough estimate of how skillful the
player is.  Next is kills per death ratio.  Third is win percentage.
Fourth is doom percent, and finally the player's class is shown.

See also:  KD, FINGER
~

0 FINGER~
Syntax:  finger self
Syntax:  finger <name>

Finger will give you a variety of information about a player,
as well as giving you an idea of the players strengths and
weaknesses.  It lists the number of kills and deaths a player
has, the players level, the amount of damage they have 
inflicted and recieved, and the class of the players they
have killed and been killed by.

See Also:  WHO, KD
~

0 LEVEL LEVELS~
There are no levels on this MUD.  All characters have the
stats of a "30th level" character on a typical Diku style MUD.

See also:  WHO, KD, FINGER
~  

0 KD Kill/Death~
Your KD or Kill/Death ratio is the number of kills you have 
made, divided by the number of times you have been killed.
This allows other players to see just how tough a player they 
are up against.  Your KD can be seen by typing score or
who.  Your kills and deaths can seen by typing score or
finger.

See Also:  WHO, FINGER
~

0 where 'free wheres'~
Syntax: where
Syntax: where <character>

WHERE without an argument tells you the location of visible players in the same
area as you are.  This is free.

WHERE with an argument tells you the location of one character with that name,
including monsters, anywhere in the world, _not only in zone_ this costs
30 coins.

The free wheres skill allows you to use the where command for free.
If you have no gold, and fail your free wheres skill, you will be
assessed with a 3 second delay.
~

0 whereis~
Syntax: whereis <room name>

WHEREIS tells you what zones have a room with the exact name (case insensitive)
that you input.  For example:

> whereis the hall
The Hall - The Keep of Mahn-Tor
> whereis hall
Hall - New Thalos
> whereis hallway
Hallway - Drow City
Hallway - Dragon Tower
Hallway - Kerofk
Hallway - Darkfriends Guild


~

0 whereload~
Syntax: whereload <obj vnum>

WHERELOAD tells you where an object loads.  For example:

> whereload 5105
Loads on mob: The weapons master
Loads on mob: Arachnos
Loads on mob: the screaming harpy leader
> whereload 2428
Loads in room: Mages' Guild Storeroom
> whereload 5638
Loads in object: the teensy little door

~

0 HELP~
Syntax: help
Syntax: help <keyword>

HELP without any arguments shows a one-page command summary.

HELP <keyword> shows a page of help on that keyword.  The keywords include
all the commands, spells, and skills listed in the game.
~

0 DESCRIPTION~
Syntax: description <string>
Syntax: description + <string>

Sets your long description to the given string.  If the description string
starts with a '+', the part after the '+' is appended to your current
description, so that you can make multi-line descriptions.

Descriptions now save!  (As long as your character is saved)
~

0 PASSWORD~
Syntax: password <old-password> <new-password>

PASSWORD changes your character's password.  The first argument must be
your old password.  The second argument is your new password.

The PASSWORD command is protected against being snooped or logged.
~

0 TITLE~
Syntax: title <string>

Sets your title to a given string.

The game supplies a title when your character is created.  You can use TITLE 
to set your title to something else.

Yeah, change your title to something slick, like:
title glows with a bright light.
title utterly annihilates you with his slash.

etc.
~

0 WIMPY~
Syntax: wimpy
Syntax: wimpy <number>

WIMPY sets your wimpy value.  When your character takes damage that reduces
your hit points below your wimpy value, you will automatically attempt to flee.

WIMPY with no argument sets your wimpy value to 20% of your maximum hit points.

Oh, btw, WIMPY doesn't really work here :)  Sorry.  Flee your own damn self.
~

0 OPEN CLOSE LOCK UNLOCK PICK~
Syntax: open   <object|direction>
Syntax: close  <object|direction>
Syntax: lock   <object|direction>
Syntax: unlock <object|direction>
Syntax: pick   <object|direction>

OPEN and CLOSE open and close an object or a door.

LOCK and UNLOCK lock and unlock a closed object or door.  You must have
the requisite key to LOCK or UNLOCK.

PICK can open a lock without having the key.  In order to PICK successfully,
you must practice the appropriate skill.
~

0 DRINK EAT FILL~
Syntax: drink <object>
Syntax: eat   <object>
Syntax: fill  <object>

Well, I kinda took food and water out.

Drink however does some rather important things, like heal you.

FILL fills a drink container with water.
~

0 BUY LIST SELL VALUE~
Syntax: buy   <object>
Syntax: list
Syntax: list  <object>
Syntax: sell  <object>
Syntax: value <object>

BUY buys an object from a shop keeper.

LIST lists the objects the shop keeper will sell you.  LIST <object> lists
just the objects with that name.

SELL sells an object to a shop keeper.

VALUE asks the shop keeper how much he, she, or it will buy the item for.
~

0 SACRIFICE~
Syntax: sacrifice <object>

SACRIFICE offers an object to your God.  God may reward you for the sacrifice.
The nature of the reward depends upon the type of object.

Hint: God likes corpses.
Hint: Rip likes player corpses, and is always watching for these.
~

0 BRANDISH QUAFF RECITE ZAP USE~
Syntax: brandish
Syntax: quaff    <potion>
Syntax: recite   <scroll> <target>
Syntax: zap      <target>
Syntax: zap
Syntax: use      <object>

BRANDISH brandishes a magical staff.  QUAFF quaffs a magical potion (as opposed
to DRINK, which drinks mundane liquids).  RECITE recites a magical scroll; the
<target> is optional, depending on the nature of the scroll.  ZAP zaps a
magical wand at a target.  If the target is not specified, and you are fighting
someone, then that character is used for a target.

You must HOLD a wand or a staff before using BRANDISH or ZAP.

All of these commands use up their objects.  Potions and scrolls have a single
charge.  Wands and staves have multiple charges.  When a magical object has no
more charges, it will be consumed.

USE has recently been added, and can be used in place of BRANDISH or ZAP.
~

0 CONSIDER~
Syntax: consider <character>

CONSIDER tells you what your chances are of killing a character.
Of course, it's only a rough estimate.
~

0 hide sneak visible~
Syntax: hide
Syntax: sneak
Syntax: visible

If you successfully HIDE, then other characters can't see you.

If you successfully SNEAK, then you can move in and out of rooms without
being noticed.  Typing sneak toggles whether you are trying to sneak.
Whether you succeed or not is checked in every room, and is based on
your sneak skill.

VISIBLE cancels your hiding and sneaking, as well as any invisibility,
making you visible again.
~

0 QUIT SAVE~
Syntax: QUIT
Syntax: SAVE

SAVE saves your character.  You must have at least two kills to
save.  The game will auto-save your character every few minutes whether you
save or not.

QUIT leaves the game.

You may not quit if you are in war.  If you cut link, you are left
in the war, and anyone that finds you can probably kill you.

There is no way to save equipment or money.  All characters are given
a lantern, minor weapon, and mediocre equipment upon login.
~

0 RECALL /~
Syntax: RECALL

RECALL prays to God for miraculous transportation from where you are back to
the Temple of Hell.  '/' is a synonym for RECALL.

RECALL doesn't work in certain God-forsaken rooms.  Characters afflicted by a
curse may not recall at all.

Recall is only available on scrolls now, or to players not in war.
~

0 STEAL~
Syntax: STEAL coins <character>
Syntax: STEAL <object> <character>

STEAL attempts to steal coins, or an object, from a character.

There are penalties for using STEAL on other players.
Yeah, like, if you steal from a player, you might like, get something
useful instead of lame stuff from mobs, ;)

Thieves can steal worn containers.  Everyone else can't.
~

0 'steal enchantment'~
Syntax: cast 'steal enchantment' <victim> <spell name>

This spell will attempt to steal a spell enchantment from the victim
and transfer it to the caster.  The victim gets a saving throw.

example:
c 'steal e' gambit sanct
~

0 PRACTICE~
Syntax: PRACTICE
Syntax: PRACTICE <skill|spell>

PRACTICE without an argument tells you your ability level in all
the skills and spells available to you.  You can check this anywhere.

It is generally impossible to practice spells on this mud.
~

40 ADVANCE~
Syntax: advance <character> <level>

ADVANCE sets a character's level to the specified value.  It is the
only way to create new immortal characters within the game.  ADVANCE
may also be used to demote characters.
~

40 TRUST~
Syntax: trust <character> <level>

TRUST sets a character's trust to the specified level.  This is similar to
advancing the character for accessing immortal commands, but has no effect
on combat strength, et cetera.  You can give more trust to mortal (or
immortal) characters to give them access to more power without messing up
the character.  You can also restrict a player's trust to less than their
natural level.

A trust of 0 means to use the character's natural level again.
~

39 ALLOW BAN~
Syntax: allow   <site>
Syntax: ban     <site>
Syntax: ban

BAN <site> prohibits players from that site from logging in.  BAN with
no arguments lists the banned sites.  ALLOW <site> removes a site from
the ban list.

The site ban test works by suffix comparison, so if you ban 'foo.edu',
all sites within 'foo.edu' are banned as well.

Site bans are not saved; every time the server is rebooted, the site
ban list is cleared.  
~

39 REBOOT SHUTDOWN WIZLOCK~
Syntax: reboot
Syntax: shutdown
Syntax: wizlock

REBOOT shuts down the server.  When the normal 'startup' script is used
to control the server, a delay of sixty seconds will ensue (to allow
old connections to die), followed by a reboot.

SHUTDOWN shuts down the server and prevents the normal 'startup' script
from restarting it.

WIZLOCK is a toggle command.  When the server is WIZLOCKed, players
below level 36 may not log in.  Players who lose their links, however,
may reconnect.
~

39 DENY DISCONNECT FREEZE~
Syntax: deny       <character>
Syntax: disconnect <character>
Syntax: freeze     <character>

DENY denies access to a particular player and throws them out of the game.
DENY is permanent (persists across reboots) and is not undoable from within the
game.  Only someone with access to the player files directory may repeal a
DENY by editing the appropriate player file.

DISCONNECT <character> immediately disconnects that character.

FREEZE <character> is a toggle which prevents a character from issuing any
commands at all.
~

39 FORCE~
Syntax: force <character> <command>
Syntax: force all <command>

FORCE forces one character to execute a command.

FORCE 'all' forces all player characters to execute a command.
This is typically used for 'force all save'.
~

0 USERS~
Syntax: users

USERS reports all of the visible users connected to the server, including users
in the process of logging in.  The two numbers inside brackets are the channel
number and the 'connected' state.  The 'connected' states are listed in merc.h:
0 is CON_PLAYING; non-zero values are various states of logging in.

The mortal version does not show the actual machine a player is connected
from, and it does not show socket numbers.
~

38 MLOAD OLOAD~
Syntax: mload <vnum>
Syntax: oload <vnum>
Syntax: oload <vnum> <level>

MLOAD loads a mobile given its vnum (virtual number).  The vnum's are just
the ##NNNN numbers that appear in world.obj.

OLOAD loads an object given its vnum.  It takes an optional parameter for
the level of the object to be loaded.
~

38 MSET OSET RSET SSET~
Syntax: mset <character> <field> <value>
Syntax: oset <object>    <field> <value>
Syntax: rset <location>  <field> <value>
Syntax: sset <character> <skill> <value>
Syntax: sset <character> all     <value>

MSET, OSET, and RSET set the properties of mobiles, objects, and rooms,
respectively.  SSET sets a skill or spell level on a PC.

There is currently no way to enter more than one line of text for a
string-valued option.
~

38 NOEMOTE NOTELL SILENCE~
Syntax: noemote <character>
Syntax: notell  <character>
Syntax: silence <character>

NOEMOTE, NOTELL, and SILENCE are used to muffle other characters, by
preventing them from emoting, telling, and using channels, respectively.
Characters who are no'telled will also not receive those forms of
communication.
~

38 PARDON~
Syntax: pardon <character> killer
Syntax: pardon <character> thief

PARDON pardons a player for their crimes.

Crimes? Those are VIRTUES, not crimes, you idiots.
~

38 PURGE~
Syntax: purge
Syntax: purge <character>

PURGE is used to clean up the world.  PURGE with no arguments removes all the
NPC's and objects in the current room.  PURGE with an argument purges one
character from anywhere in the world.

PURGE will not get rid of PC's.
~

38 RESTORE~
Syntax: restore <character>

RESTORE restores full hit points, mana points, and movement points to the
target character.
~

38 SLAY~
Syntax: slay <character>

SLAY kills a character in cold blood, no saving throw.

SLAY bypasses auto actions such as AUTOLOOT and AUTOSAC.
~

0 TRANSFER~
Syntax: transfer <character>
Syntax: transfer all
Syntax: transfer <character> <location>
Syntax: transfer all         <location>

TRANSFER transfers the target character, or ALL player characters,
to your current location (default) or to a specified location.

Mortals may transfer other mortals now, there are a few rules of
course:
	no transing mobs
	no transing players in war
	no transing if your in war
	no transing players that do not have trans on

You can turn on trans by typing:  trans 1
You can turn off trans by typing: trans 0

~


37 AT~
Syntax: at <location> <command>

AT executes the given command (which may have arguments) at the given
location.  The location may be specified as a vnum, as the name of
a mobile, or as the name of an object.

AT works by temporarily moving you to that location, executing the
command, and then moving you back (if the command didn't change your
location).
~

37 ECHO RECHO~
Syntax: echo  <message>
Syntax: recho <message>

ECHO sends a message to all players in the game, without any prefix
indicating who sent it.

RECHO sends a message to all players in your room, without any prefix
indicating who sent it.
~

0 GOTO~
Syntax: goto <location>

GOTO takes you to a location.  The location may be specified as a vnum,
as the name of a mobile, or as the name of an object.

You may not GOTO a room if it is PRIVATE and has two (or more) characters
already present, or if it is SOLITARY and has one (or more) characters
already present.

The mortal version of GOTO only works on NPCs, and only while you are
out of the war.
~

37 HOLYLIGHT INVIS~
Syntax: holylight
Syntax: invis

HOLYLIGHT is a toggle that allows you to see (almost) every character and
mobile in the game.  With holy light, you can see what's in the game more
easily.  Without holy light, you can see what mortal players see.

INVIS is a toggle which makes you completely invisible to all player characters
of lower level than you, and to all mobiles.

HOLYLIGHT will not allow you to see immortal INVIS characters who are
higher level than you.
~

37 MEMORY~
Syntax: memory

MEMORY reports the size of your mud.

The 'Mobs' and 'Objs' lines report the number of types of mobiles and objects,
not the number of instances currently in the game.

The 'Strings' line shows memory used by area-file strings.  These strings have
a maximum limit, which is also shown.

The 'Perms' line shows permanently allocated memory blocks.  There is no limit
on the number and size of these blocks.
~

37 MFIND~
Syntax: mfind <name>

MFIND finds all mobile types with a particular name.
~

0 OFIND VOSTAT~
Syntax: ofind <name>
Syntax: vostat <vnumber>

OFIND finds all object vnums for a specific keyword.

VOSTAT identifies the prototype for an object's vnum.

This is useful for finding and identifying objects that are NOT in
your possession!
~

0 osearch~
Syntax: osearch [vnum <number>] [wear <location>] [affect <aff>]
                [type <item type>]

osearch is a more powerful version of ofind.

osearch can be used to find damage roll items worn on the head,
osearch wear head affect damroll
or perhaps resist lightning items:
osearch affect 'resist lightning'
or lights which affect wisdom
osearch type light affect wisdom

The vnum argument specifies what vnum you want to start searching
from.  osearch will return a maximum of 20 matches.

This command is not yet complete, and more options will be added.

and so on.
~

37 MSTAT OSTAT RSTAT~
Syntax: mstat <character>
Syntax: ostat <object>
Syntax: rstat <location>
Syntax: rstat

MSTAT shows you statistics on a character.

OSTAT shows you statistics on an object.

RSTAT shows you statistics on a location. The location may be specified as
a vnum, as the name of a mobile, or as the name of an object.
~

37 MWHERE~
Syntax: mwhere <name>

MWHERE shows you the locations of all mobiles with a particular name.
~

37 PEACE~
Syntax: peace

PEACE causes all characters in a room to stop fighting.
~

37 BAMFIN BAMFOUT~
Syntax: bamfin  <message>
Syntax: bamfout <message>

The standard messages for GOTO are 'arrives in a swirling mist' and
'leaves in a swirling mist'.  You can replace these messages with messages of
your own choosing using the BAMFIN and BAMFOUT commands.
~

37 SLOOKUP~
Syntax: slookup <skill-or-spell>
Syntax: slookup all

SLOOKUP shows you the internal 'sn' and external 'slot' for the given skill
or spell.  The 'sn' is used for OSET and OSTAT.  The 'slot' is used for area
files.  Neither 'sn' nor 'slot' is used for player files; these operate with
the actual skill and spell names.

SLOOKUP ALL shows this information for all skills and spells.
~

37 LOG~
Syntax: log   <character>
Syntax: log   all

LOG <character> causes all of a character's commands to be logged into
the server's log file.  Like FREEZE, it is a permanent character toggle.

Certain commands (PASSWORD) are protected against LOG.  Conversely, certain
commands (especially immortal commands) are always logged.

LOG ALL logs all character commands.  It is a server-wide toggle; it does not
persist across reboots.
~

0 SNOOP~
Syntax: snoop <character>

This command allows mortals and immortals to watch characters through
the character's eyes.  Mortals are restricted to using snoop while they
are out of the war, and the victim is in the war.

Using snoop as a mortal causes you to be unable to communicate until
the end of the current war, and you automatically stop snooping people
when they become out of war.

Snoop yourself to stop all snoops.

Abuse of this command through methods such as logging on a second character
to gossip gained information will result in bad things...
~

37 SWITCH RETURN~
Syntax: switch <character>
Syntax: return

SWITCH switches you into the body of another character (who must not
already have a descriptor, e.g. you can't switch into a PC).

RETURN returns you to your original body.
~

36 IMMTALK :~
Syntax: immtalk <message>

IMMTALK sends a message to all immortals.  : is a synonym for IMMTALK.
~
36 WIZHELP~
Syntax: wizhelp

WIZHELP provides a list of all the immortal commands.
~

0 NOPLAY~
Syntax: noplay

This command will toggle your no_play flag.
If you are set noplay, you will not play in any war until reboot, or until
you type noplay again.

~

0 ARMOR~
Syntax: cast armor <character>

This spell decreases (improves) the armor class of the target character
by 20 points.
~

0 'banish item'~
Syntax: cast 'banish item' <victim> <keyword>

This spell will attempt to destroy every object with the given
keyword carried or worn by the victim.  The victim gets a saving
throw for each item to be destroyed.

Example:
c banish levias blood
c banish time eog
c banish onion potion
c banish borlak bronze
~

0 BLACKMANTLE~
Syntax: cast blackmantle <victim>

This spell renders the victim immune (heh) to all forms of healing.
~

0 BLESS~
Syntax: cast bless <character>

This spell improves the to-hit roll and saving throw versus spell of the
target character by +1 each.
~

0 BLINDNESS~
Syntax: cast blindness <victim>

This spell renders the target character blind.
~

0 BRILLIANCE~
Syntax: cast brilliance

This spell significantly improves the intelligence of the caster for
a very brief time.
~

0 'BURNING HANDS' 'COLOUR SPRAY' FIREBALL 'LIGHTNING BOLT'
  'MAGIC MISSILE' 'SHOCKING GRASP'~
Syntax: cast 'burning hands'  <victim>
Syntax: cast 'colour spray'   <victim>
Syntax: cast 'fireball'       <victim>
Syntax: cast 'lightning bolt' <victim>
Syntax: cast 'magic missile'  <victim>
Syntax: cast 'shocking grasp' <victim>

These spells inflict damage on the victim.  

~

0 'CALL LIGHTNING'~
Syntax: cast 'call lightning'

This spell works only out of doors, and only when the weather is bad.
It calls down lightning bolts from God.
~

0 'CAUSE LIGHT' 'CAUSE SERIOUS' 'CAUSE CRITICAL' HARM~
Syntax: cast 'cause light'    <victim>
Syntax: cast 'cause serious'  <victim>
Syntax: cast 'cause critical' <victim>
Syntax: cast harm             <victim>

These spells inflict damage on the victim.  The higher-level spells do
more damage.
~

0 'CHARM PERSON'~
Syntax: cast 'charm person' <victim>

This spell, if successful, causes the victim to follow you and to take orders
from you.  Use ORDER to order your charmed followers.

It is more difficult to charm more than one pet, and additional
charmees might take offense at your attempt to charm them.
~

0 'CHILL TOUCH'~
Syntax: cast 'chill touch' <victim>

This spell inflicts damage on the victim and also reduces the victim's
strength by three.
~

0 'CONTINUAL LIGHT'~
Syntax: cast 'continual light'

This spell creates a ball of light, which you can hold as a light source.
The ball of light will last indefinitely.
~

0 'CONTROL WEATHER'~
Syntax: cast 'control weather' better
Syntax: cast 'control weather' worse

This spell makes the weather either better or worse.

Here's how it works.

Type weather.  It says the current state, and either a cold northern
or a warm southern breeze.  If it's cold, the weather will get a little
worse next tick.  If it says warm, it will get a little better.

You cast control once per tick, then wait.  Changes aren't automatic,
and it could take several ticks to see a change.

If you're counting on rain, you better start casting from the first
tick of the war.
~

0 'CREATE FOOD'~
Syntax: cast 'create food'

This spell creates a Magic Mushroom, which you or anyone else can eat.
~

0 'create illusory blood'~
Syntax: cast 'create illusory blood'

This spell creates a false blood, which you can give to a player
to confuse them.
~

0 'CREATE SPRING'~
Syntax: cast 'create spring'

This spell brings forth a magical spring from the ground, which has the
same properties as a fountain.
~

0 'CREATE WATER'~
Syntax: cast 'create water' <drink-container>

This spell replenishes a drink container with water.
~

0 'CURE BLINDNESS'~
Syntax: cast 'cure blindness' <character>

This spell cures blindness in one so unfortunate.
~

0 'CURE POISON'~
Syntax: cast 'cure poison' <character>

This spell cures poison in one so unfortunate.
~

0 'CURE LIGHT' 'CURE SERIOUS' 'CURE CRITICAL' HEAL MIRACLE~
Syntax: cast 'cure light'    <character>
Syntax: cast 'cure serious'  <character>
Syntax: cast 'cure critical' <character>
Syntax: cast 'heal'          <character>

These spells cure damage on the target character.  The higher-level spells
heal more damage.  Miracle restores the character to full hit points.
~

0 CURSE~
Syntax: cast 'curse' <character>

This spell reduces the character's to-hit roll by 1 and save versus spells
by 1.  It also renders the character unclean in the eyes of God and
unable to RECALL.
~

0 'DANCING SWORD'~
Syntax: cast 'dancing sword' <sword>

This spell will animate a sword into motion.  The sword will follow
the caster on it's own will, and if the caster is in combat, it will
attack the object of the caster's aggression.

The damage of the sword is based on the quality of the sword enchanted
(the damage dice are identical).

The enchantment does not last forever, and the weapon is destroyed
when it terminates.  How long it lasts is based on the weight, as is
the speed at which the weapon attacks.

Note that the weapon does not follow particularly fast, and for this
spell to be effective, the caster should make sure he is reasonably
near his victim before casting.

The enchantment placed upon the sword is not especially strong,
and either physical or magical damage to the weapon can end it sooner.
~

0 'DEATH SPRAY'~
Syntax: cast 'death spray'

Room affect, nice, real nice.
~

0 'DETECT EVIL'~
Syntax: cast 'detect evil'

This spell enables the caster to detect evil characters, which will
reveal a characteristic red aura.
~

0 'DETECT HIDDEN'~
Syntax: cast 'detect hidden'

This spell enables the caster to detect hidden creatures.
~

0 'DETECT INVIS'~
Syntax: cast 'detect invis'

This spell enables the caster to detect invisible objects and characters.
~

0 'DETECT MAGIC'~
Syntax: cast 'detect magic'

This spell enables the caster to detect magical objects.
~

0 'DETECT POISON'~
Syntax: cast 'detect poison' <object>

This spell detects the presence of poison in food or drink.
~

0 'DISPEL EVIL'~
Syntax: cast 'dispel evil' <victim>

This spell invokes the wrath of God on an evil victim.

Modified back to a more standard diku damage amount...
~

0 'DISPEL MAGIC'~
Syntax: cast 'dispel magic' <character>

This spell dispels magical affects on a character.  It may be used as
an offensive spell to remove an enemy's magical advantages, or as a
benign spell to remove a friend's (or one's own) magical handicaps.
~


0 EARTHQUAKE~
Syntax: cast 'earthquake'

This spell inflicts damage on every enemy character in the room.
Beware that other characters who are not yet fighting may attack
you as a result!
~

0 'ENCHANT WEAPON'~
Syntax: cast 'enchant weapon' <weapon>

This spell magically enchants a weapon, increasing its to-hit and to-dam
bonuses.  The weapon must be un-magical to start with.

The enchanter class gets a bonus to enchanting weapons.
~

0 'ENERGY DRAIN'~
Syntax: cast 'energy drain' <victim>

This spell saps the mana from the victim and gives it to the caster.
In addition, it generates a nominal amount of damage.
~

0 entangle~
Syntax: cast 'entangle' <victim>

This spell causes the earth to spew roots from the ground, binding the
victim in place until he or she breaks free.

In addition, this makes the victim easier to hit, by virtue of their
not being very mobile.

Druids cast this spell twice as powerful as any other class.
~

0 'FAERIE FIRE'~
Syntax: cast 'faerie fire' <victim>

This spell increases (makes worse) the armor class of its victim.  For each
level of the caster, the victim's armor class is increased by two points.

Players affected by faerie fire do not hide very well.
~

0 'FAERIE FOG'~
Syntax: cast 'faerie fog'

This spell reveals all manner of invisible, hidden, and sneaking creatures in
the same room as you.
~

0 FEEBLEMIND~
Syntax: cast 'feeblemind' <victim>

This spell reduces the intelligence of the victim for some time.
~

0 FLAMESTRIKE 'WHIRLING WIND'~
Syntax: cast 'flamestrike' <victim>
Syntax: cast 'whirling wind' <victim>

This spell inflicts damage on the victim.

Whirling wind does slightly less damage than flamestrike, but does
double against a flying opponent, and knocks them from the air.
~

0 FLY~
Syntax: cast 'fly' <character>

This spell enables the target character to fly.
~

0 'GIANT STRENGTH'~
Syntax: cast 'giant strength' <character>

This spell increases the strength of the target character.

It increases the strength of mages by more than others.
~

0 IDENTIFY~
Syntax: cast identify <object>

This spell reveals information about the object.
~


0 'ILLUSORY APPEARANCE'~
Syntax: cast 'illusory appearance'

This spell makes the caster appear as if he were a beastly fido
while he is moving.
~


0 'ILLUSORY AURAS'~
Syntax: cast 'illusory auras'

This makes the caster appear as if he were surrounded by various
auras, occasionally.  These auras could include sanct, blackmantle,
void aura, etc.
~


0 INFRAVISION~
Syntax: cast infravision <character>

This spell enables the target character to see in the dark.
~

0 INVIS 'MASS INVIS' 'IMPROVED INVIS'~
Syntax: cast invis <character>
Syntax: cast 'mass invis'

The INVIS spell makes the target character invisible.  Invisible characters
will become visible when they attack.

The MASS INVIS spell makes all characters in the caster's group invisible,
including the caster.

IMPROVED INVIS characters are really really hard to find :)
You do not regenerate mana while improved invis.
~

0 'KNOW ALIGNMENT'~
Syntax: cast 'know alignment' <character>

This spell reveals the alignment of the target character.
~

0 'LOCATE OBJECT'~
Syntax: cast 'locate object' <name>

This spell reveals the location of all objects with the given name.
~

0 mesmerize~
Syntax: cast 'mesmerize' <victim>

This spell makes the victim stare at the sky for a short
amount of time.
~

0 'PASS DOOR'~
Syntax: cast 'pass door'

This spell enables the caster to pass through closed doors.
~

0 'personal illusion'~
Syntax: cast 'personal illusion'

This spell creates an illusion of the caster.

The illusion can be destroyed by combat, and it is not especially sturdy.
~

0 'phantasmal weaponry'~
Syntax: cast 'phantasmal weaponry'

This gives the caster the appearance of having much better combat
abilities than he or she truly has.
~

0 POISON~
Syntax: cast poison <victim>

This spell reduces the strength of the victim by two, as well as reducing the
victim's regeneration rate.
~

0 PROTECTION SANCTUARY FIRESHIELD~
Syntax: cast protection
Syntax: cast sanctuary  <character>

The PROTECTION spell reduces the damage taken from any attack by an evil
creature by one quarter.

The SANCTUARY spell reduces the damage taken by the character from any attack
by one half.

FIRESHIELD provides no protection, but the attacker will take as much
damage as he inflicts with physical attacks.  Note that fireshield damage
is taken BEFORE normal damage, thus if an assassin backstabs someone for
the last kill... guess who wins?

These spells may be used simultaneously for cumulative effect.
~

0 FARSEE~
Syntax: cast farsee

The farsee spell allows the magic user to see far into the distance.
ie: it will allow him to look into the next room.

Read help look for more information.
~

0 reflect~
Syntax: cast reflect

This spell causes the next offensive spell cast at the caster to
be redirected at the person casting it.
~

0 'REMOVE CURSE'~
Syntax: cast 'remove curse' <character>

This spell removes a curse from a character.
~

0 SHIELD 'STONE SKIN'~
Syntax: cast shield
Syntax: cast 'stone skin'

These spells protect the caster by decreasing (improving) the caster's armor
class.  SHIELD provides 20 points of armor.  STONE SKIN provides 40 points of
armor.
~

0 silence~
Syntax: cast silence <victim>

This spell prevents the victim from casting spells.
~


0 slow~
Syntax: cast slow <victim>

This reduces the speed of the victim's body, eliminating any additional
attacks, and making them travel slower.
~


0 SUMMON~
Syntax: cast summon <character>

This spell summons a mob from anywhere else in your zone into your room.
Characters may not be summoned unless the summon war flag is on.


~

0 TELEPORT~
Syntax: cast <teleport>

This spell takes you from your current location to a random location somewhere
in the world.


Syntax:  teleport

This only works often if you are dead, it teleports you to a random
location in the world...

Every player now receives a single teleport per war that may be used
during the first tick, to prevent "bad zone" complaints.
~

0 'VAMPIRIC TOUCH'~
Syntax: cast 'vampiric touch' <victim>

This spell saps the hit points of it's target and gives them to
the caster.

Necromancers cause twice as much damage with this spell, and cast
the spell as if they were 4 levels higher.
~

0 VENTRILOQUATE~
Syntax: cast ventriloquate <speaker> <message>

This spell throws your voice, making it appear that some other object or
character in the room is saying your message.  Victims who make their saving
throw will know that someone is using ventriloquism, but not who.  Victims who
fail their saving throw will think that the object or character really did say
your message.
~

0 'void aura'~
Syntax: cast 'void aura'

This spell creates an aura about the caster comprised of material from
the negative material plane.  Anything touching the caster will destroy
both itself and the aura.
~

0 WEAKEN~
Syntax: cast weaken <victim>

This spell reduces the strength of the victim by two points.
~

0 'weapon immunity'~
Syntax: cast 'weapon immunity'

This spell makes the caster highly resistant to physical damage.
~

0 'WIZARD EYE'~
Syntax: cast 'wizard eye' <victim>

This spell gives the caster a close up view of the victim,
from anywhere in the game.
~

0 'WORD OF RECALL'~
Syntax: cast 'word of recall'

This spell takes the caster to the cathedral in Ys.
~

0 'ACID BREATH' 'FIRE BREATH' 'FROST BREATH' 'GAS BREATH' 'LIGHTNING BREATH'~
Syntax: cast 'acid breath'      <victim>
Syntax: cast 'fire breath'      <victim>
Syntax: cast 'frost breath'     <victim>
Syntax: cast 'gas breath'
Syntax: cast 'lightning breath' <victim>

These spells are for the use of dragons.  Acid, fire, frost, and lightning
damage one victim, whereas gas damages every PC in the room.  Fire and
frost can break objects, and acid can damage armor.

Note that breath damage is based on the caster's current hit points.
Thus, if you are going to use breath, use it early, and collect hit
point eqiupment.
~

0 DODGE~

This skill lets you dodge incoming attacks, taking no damage from them.
Use of the skill is automatic.
~

0 'ENHANCED DAMAGE'~

This skill increases the damage you inflict when attacking.  Use of the skill
is automatic once you have practice it.
~

0 PARRY~

This skill wards off incoming attacks, taking no damage from them.  Use of the
skill is automatic once you have practice it.  You must be wielding a weapon
to parry.
~

0 PEEK~

This skill shows you a character's inventory when you look at a character.
Use of the skill is automatic.

This skill will also let you look into the next room, read help look.
~

0 'SECOND ATTACK' 'THIRD ATTACK'~

These skills let you attack more than once during a combat round.  Use of
these skills is automatic once you have practice them.

You can practice THIRD ATTACK before you have mastered SECOND ATTACK,
although it isn't efficient to do so.
~

